Implement transitionFromViewController:toViewController:options:completionHandler:, which was missing.

A container swaps one screenful for another with this method, and cocotron did not have it at all.
A missing method here is SILENT in the worst way: the selector is sent, the receiver does not
recognise it, and an application that catches exceptions carries on with the swap simply not done.
iA Writer catches, which is how cocotron 0031 was found.

The selector IS SENT by iA Writer, not merely mentioned: it appears in __TEXT,__objc_methname,
which is where a sent selector lives, spelled
transitionFromViewController:toViewController:options:completionHandler:.

THIS DOES NOT FIX #194, and the run says so plainly. With the method implemented and traced, the
scenario that leaves the editor pane empty calls it ZERO times, in a run that is otherwise healthy:
2 pointer events, 50 viewDidLoad lines and 894 CIDER_VIEW lines, so the gate is on and the
instrument speaks. iA Writer swaps its editor some other way. The selector is in the binary because
something else in the application uses it.

It is committed anyway because the gap is real: any application that does call it was getting
nothing, and finding that out later from a silent symptom is expensive.

THE ANIMATION IS NOT PERFORMED. macOS animates the swap; the contract is that the new view ends up
in the old one's place, inherits its frame and autoresizing mask, and the completion handler runs.
Doing it immediately keeps that contract, and animating it is a separate question from being
correct. NSViewControllerTransitionOptions is declared with the documented values so callers
compile, and ignored.

